# No hardcoded primary-key IDs

Do **not** introduce new literal primary-key identities in Python tests or app code.

**Forbidden in `tests/` (AST-guarded by `tests/test_hardcoded_ids.py`):**
- `User(id=<int>)`
- `user_id=<int>` as a Call keyword with an integer constant (including
  `ReadingSnapshot(user_id=1)`, `replace(..., user_id=2)`, ORM constructors)

**Forbidden in `app/`:** new `user_id=1` literals.

**Required instead:**
- Database-generated IDs from fixtures (`default_user.id`, `get_or_create_user_async`,
  flushed ORM `.id` values)
- For pure in-memory evaluator snapshots that need stable reader keys, pass
  `user_id=default_user.id` (or another fixture-owned id). Do **not** write
  `user_id=1` / `user_id=2` even as “fixture constants” in Call keywords — the
  guard sees those AST literals. Module-level names only help when the Call uses
  the name (`user_id=READER_ID`), never the bare integer.

**Do not expand** `tests/hardcoded_ids_baseline.json`. Fix the new literal or shrink
an existing baseline entry when removing one.

**Migration / recovery fixtures:** production Step 14+ classifications are keyed by
real dependency IDs. Synthetic Dependency rows must not inherit those lookups when
auto-IDs collide (isolate with an explicit test double, or advance sequences past
the classification map). See `tests/test_reader_order_migration_coordinator.py`.

**Local gate:** `bash scripts/check-python-ci-lint.sh` runs
`pytest tests/test_hardcoded_ids.py` after ruff/ty. A green ruff/ty-only run is not
enough after adding or editing Python tests.
